values ('a2b3c4d5-6e7f-4b02-9c12-3d4e5f6a7b22', 'sociology-of-the-family', 'Sociology of the Family', 'This course frames family as a social institution, intimate relationship system, legal category, cultural ideal, and site of inequality. Definitions of family shape law, benefits, identity, obligation, and belonging, and family forms have always changed even when public debate imagines a stable traditional past. The focus runs through data literacy, intimacy and marriage, divorce and repartnering, parenting, gendered care work, class, race and migration, LGBTQ+ kinship, varied households, violence and safety, family policy, cross-cultural variation, aging and caregiving, technology and reproductive futures, and using family research responsibly in public debate.');

