  'Sociology of the Family',
  'Sociology of the Family',
  'This course frames family as a social institution, intimate relationship system, legal category, cultural ideal, and site of inequality. Definitions of family shape law, benefits, identity, obligation, and belonging, and family forms have always changed even when public debate imagines a stable traditional past. The focus runs through data literacy, intimacy and marriage, divorce and repartnering, parenting, gendered care work, class, race and migration, LGBTQ+ kinship, varied households, violence and safety, family policy, cross-cultural variation, aging and caregiving, technology and reproductive futures, and using family research responsibly in public debate.',
  $sofamjson${"slug": "sociology-of-the-family", "title": "Sociology of the Family", "description": "This course frames family as a social institution, intimate relationship system, legal category, cultural ideal, and site of inequality. Definitions of family shape law, benefits, identity, obligation, and belonging, and family forms have always changed even when public debate imagines a stable traditional past. The focus runs through data literacy, intimacy and marriage, divorce and repartnering, parenting, gendered care work, class, race and migration, LGBTQ+ kinship, varied households, violence and safety, family policy, cross-cultural variation, aging and caregiving, technology and reproductive futures, and using family research responsibly in public debate.", "topics": [{"id": "11a192f4-e2bd-4674-86ea-a2e34f7472be", "type": "topic", "title": "The Sociological Study of Family", "description": "This opening topic frames family as a social institution, intimate relationship system, legal category, cultural ideal, and site of inequality. Students learn to move beyond personal experience toward evidence-based sociological analysis.", "children": [{"id": "563527ee-c7eb-4c96-9162-a333861010fc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Family as institution and lived experience", "children": [{"id": "93374f39-87ce-464a-8b3e-f336ea0338a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Private troubles and public issues"}, {"id": "ffbbdf8a-0671-4c0e-9bdc-1cafb625d4e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Family as social institution"}, {"id": "09aaf51e-760e-4433-9e0d-4b95e6092ba7", "type": "concept", "title": "Micro, meso, and macro levels"}], "description": "Students learn why family is both deeply personal and publicly organized by law, labor markets, culture, and policy."}, {"id": "da7b3905-82b0-435f-a08d-3135144f4e0e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Major theoretical lenses", "children": [{"id": "5512dad5-4ee7-4cdf-ac73-f423f437fb22", "type": "concept", "title": "Functionalism and social reproduction"}, {"id": "b712cff6-2897-43ba-aceb-2e256e40bfa9", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict and feminist theories"}, {"id": "ba7df8ec-e4f6-48d9-9948-9473c092a086", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbolic interactionism and life-course theory"}], "description": "Theory gives students competing ways to explain family stability, conflict, meaning, and change."}, {"id": "7b86d74c-1702-4d00-8890-8d98b67d2be8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence and family research", "children": [{"id": "d9ecdc98-ada3-44c7-bc5f-d4519bf06eee", "type": "concept", "title": "Quantitative family demography"}, {"id": "4ec573bd-15ec-49f6-a745-426eab83b482", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualitative family narratives"}, {"id": "eb8655c4-69b3-4f09-8b4d-d9354e9eef3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics and sensitive topics"}], "description": "Students learn how sociologists study families through surveys, interviews, ethnography, administrative data, and historical evidence."}]}, {"id": "db413992-3606-453d-8ea1-ef9067ecf8b5", "type": "topic", "title": "Defining Family, Kinship, and Household", "description": "Definitions of family shape law, benefits, identity, obligation, and belonging. This topic helps students see family as a contested category rather than a single natural form.", "children": [{"id": "8eed1fc7-27eb-4962-b220-844e89cb8222", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Family, household, and kinship", "children": [{"id": "6b19948e-c38b-4272-ae00-7d3d07def94a", "type": "concept", "title": "Biological, legal, and social kin"}, {"id": "2594059c-d0bd-43a0-9f2f-574cb117dc09", "type": "concept", "title": "Households and co-residence"}, {"id": "72d9acb3-7bd7-4b50-933d-0fcffb5db7bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Chosen and fictive kin"}], "description": "Students distinguish related but different concepts that are often collapsed in everyday speech."}, {"id": "f6f188c1-8668-42c7-9c26-de2f5c9c56fe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Norms and boundary-making", "children": [{"id": "62d1a587-684f-4768-98d2-35f18ffdeab4", "type": "concept", "title": "Nuclear-family ideal"}, {"id": "5c55e1c7-6b3b-41ce-b996-4d863266aafa", "type": "concept", "title": "Legitimacy and stigma"}, {"id": "7431cb18-f932-4348-9646-9e3d323d96e3", "type": "concept", "title": "State and institutional classification"}], "description": "Definitions include some relationships while excluding others from recognition and resources."}, {"id": "b8ffbdd5-b9db-4ca3-ad9e-24e6684d887f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Measuring family", "children": [{"id": "e56c4b3f-26ea-43bd-9a25-19062ec32ef2", "type": "concept", "title": "Household surveys"}, {"id": "912460b2-fb57-4146-88ae-3a8980014017", "type": "concept", "title": "Relationship categories"}, {"id": "4c92a5ea-43b9-413c-9417-bca8974aac72", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of official data"}], "description": "Students learn how categories used in surveys and censuses shape what researchers can see."}]}, {"id": "f7e3dd3d-76ce-4dd4-9d6a-b174c5a90edf", "type": "topic", "title": "Historical Change and Family Myths", "description": "Family forms have always changed, but public debate often imagines a stable traditional past. Students learn to evaluate nostalgia, myth, and evidence about family change.", "children": [{"id": "ad53b633-3653-4b22-ad6a-0ee559d1dfbb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical diversity of family forms", "children": [{"id": "597d94ec-65c2-4228-83ad-871f6c75e3d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Extended households"}, {"id": "61e971a9-827b-4066-8074-bda1c15a6897", "type": "concept", "title": "Apprenticeship, servitude, and domestic labor"}, {"id": "839e83aa-f887-487b-8e70-4e8d26f7a333", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrialization and separate spheres"}], "description": "Students examine how family structures shifted with economy, religion, law, migration, and demographic change."}, {"id": "a2fafac9-b3ce-45ee-9500-d5b22415941c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The traditional family myth", "children": [{"id": "1c0f6256-c314-44ac-b565-103449845edb", "type": "concept", "title": "The 1950s family ideal"}, {"id": "605c6f89-39bf-495c-9253-ff47337d3004", "type": "concept", "title": "Stephanie Coontz and family nostalgia"}, {"id": "b42e422d-b8fc-430a-b562-1ec5f39123c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, class, and exclusion in family ideals"}], "description": "Historical evidence complicates claims that one family model is timeless or universal."}, {"id": "4c4e4b92-9c7d-487e-b259-e02c028ccd69", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernization and demographic transition", "children": [{"id": "7264f8ee-04d4-4cc1-831e-d98a520a97ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Declining fertility"}, {"id": "06a025fe-d394-422c-a19c-1e9eb38c8d29", "type": "concept", "title": "Rising life expectancy"}, {"id": "a7214693-1904-4822-823a-11613c52cafe", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage timing and household size"}], "description": "Students connect fertility, mortality, education, and labor-market change to family transformation."}]}, {"id": "48d1d99a-a750-40c9-ac0c-25b9a45da22a", "type": "topic", "title": "Family Demography and Data Literacy", "description": "Family sociology depends on careful use of data. Students learn to interpret trends in marriage, divorce, fertility, cohabitation, household composition, and child wellbeing without overstating what the numbers prove.", "children": [{"id": "34d2b110-f291-4a21-8c9b-d2f28acb13e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Core demographic indicators", "children": [{"id": "05eca7a0-af6a-4173-8672-907bceb27443", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage and divorce rates"}, {"id": "aa01a40c-e0b2-40a9-b28f-826025d6f30c", "type": "concept", "title": "Fertility and birth timing"}, {"id": "b63a1e37-a2dc-4e59-80a5-6cf4e3ab1bc3", "type": "concept", "title": "Household and living-arrangement measures"}], "description": "Indicators help students compare family change across time, place, and social groups."}, {"id": "a7de0978-a427-487a-839f-924d1ff39a71", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Data sources and limitations", "children": [{"id": "31374301-070d-4072-91be-0e2ed64fc6a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Census and American Community Survey"}, {"id": "3e6e4138-53ea-4396-9b29-441345fd6f73", "type": "concept", "title": "Longitudinal surveys"}, {"id": "588fc20e-e813-485a-b0e1-c402d28cc48b", "type": "concept", "title": "Administrative and qualitative data"}], "description": "Students learn where family data comes from and what each source misses."}, {"id": "0a875116-5e46-4481-8de2-15b4b0f7900b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading family statistics critically", "children": [{"id": "1c6935ce-3b67-4e0b-b17c-dceba4d7241b", "type": "concept", "title": "Rates versus counts"}, {"id": "69c48da3-3312-4441-b138-fc64852d1a94", "type": "concept", "title": "Selection effects"}, {"id": "3193802f-aaf4-4514-8965-051b376fb8ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Correlation and causation"}], "description": "Data literacy prevents moral panic and simplistic causal claims."}]}, {"id": "ea62c89a-56e0-4e46-b6ba-0c008ae86324", "type": "topic", "title": "Intimacy, Dating, Cohabitation, and Marriage", "description": "Intimate relationships are shaped by emotion, culture, technology, economics, gender expectations, and law. Students study marriage and partnership as social arrangements that change over time.", "children": [{"id": "9afadc49-bb3d-42b1-90d6-9b443910e6e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dating and partner selection", "children": [{"id": "70bae7f5-c1f9-426b-8f62-28399eaf164a", "type": "concept", "title": "Assortative mating"}, {"id": "bf9787f0-7477-4fb9-a33e-edf45459f252", "type": "concept", "title": "Online dating and algorithms"}, {"id": "0c2be20b-137c-41c9-a58a-76d504a115d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, class, religion, and education in partner choice"}], "description": "Students examine how intimacy is patterned by social networks, markets, norms, and inequality."}, {"id": "1b7c1ea2-7ace-41f4-8a52-8956e0faa76a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cohabitation and relationship sequencing", "children": [{"id": "76c74157-bda1-4e5f-b8de-154c9f81fd74", "type": "concept", "title": "Cohabitation as trial marriage"}, {"id": "815ea71d-c259-43a7-97c7-3e422e5cbd0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic constraints"}, {"id": "63933500-3c25-463c-8421-9d2d29507364", "type": "concept", "title": "Relationship progression and ambiguity"}], "description": "Cohabitation has become a major relationship form with different meanings across groups."}, {"id": "778928d4-64ab-4f0f-9709-1e43450f2e2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Marriage as institution", "children": [{"id": "358d47ce-78a6-47ae-93a5-c39604a316c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal rights and obligations"}, {"id": "bddb5a0a-f35f-4dfe-8adc-0a4a9bca0bc4", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage markets"}, {"id": "e15f951c-7cab-446f-bca9-e64c3f30192b", "type": "concept", "title": "Changing marriage expectations"}], "description": "Marriage links intimacy to law, property, status, benefits, and cultural recognition."}]}, {"id": "a5b1b0e3-2abb-4c37-aff5-8c586e23ed63", "type": "topic", "title": "Divorce, Separation, Repartnering, and Stepfamilies", "description": "Divorce and repartnering reshape kinship, parenting, economics, and emotional life. Students learn to analyze separation without moral panic or simplistic assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "a087b109-8e91-4853-a460-7fe049158587", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Divorce trends and causes", "children": [{"id": "ee34d038-b08c-456e-bfcc-42ea65986e3f", "type": "concept", "title": "No-fault divorce"}, {"id": "3c536306-a615-45c0-87b8-d23f76db8578", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic strain"}, {"id": "1e424365-b399-4efc-9c71-df5119b3f615", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender roles and relationship quality"}], "description": "Students study divorce as a patterned social process influenced by economics, gender, culture, law, and expectations."}, {"id": "688d2494-0b9d-410d-b464-0b02fa0107cf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Consequences for adults and children", "children": [{"id": "69aa646b-190b-4357-a812-5770890d77bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic consequences"}, {"id": "899a3f0a-d553-4d7d-bdd7-4fc6557d753e", "type": "concept", "title": "Child adjustment debates"}, {"id": "5159e68b-cb6d-4149-bb8a-86f66e864f30", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict versus divorce effects"}], "description": "Outcomes depend on conflict, resources, stability, parenting, and support systems."}, {"id": "0c0d1464-3a53-457e-8924-7da936a58b98", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Repartnering and stepfamilies", "children": [{"id": "3b02b704-a012-404f-b710-df09a4996148", "type": "concept", "title": "Blended families"}, {"id": "d60747bf-70ae-4006-b3ca-22eb20ae15af", "type": "concept", "title": "Co-parenting across households"}, {"id": "fa565a90-25e3-4f30-8c1a-9e630659480c", "type": "concept", "title": "Step-parent roles and boundaries"}], "description": "New partnerships create complex kinship obligations and household forms."}]}, {"id": "acdf5c90-5ad4-4fb4-9a5d-8053e8619508", "type": "topic", "title": "Parenting, Childhood, and Socialization", "description": "Families are central sites of socialization, caregiving, discipline, identity formation, and opportunity. Students connect parenting practices to culture, class, policy, and child development.", "children": [{"id": "b8188528-7830-46de-8b27-de1ad375d711", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parenting styles and practices", "children": [{"id": "909267f3-c45e-4b5b-b956-b5a8f5a1b6e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Authoritative, authoritarian, permissive, and neglectful models"}, {"id": "61a70905-4701-47cf-a4e0-d716735e8753", "type": "concept", "title": "Concerted cultivation and natural growth"}, {"id": "78b7c476-8d7a-4bed-9504-2b31d359e4ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Intensive parenting"}], "description": "Students learn that parenting norms are culturally and historically situated."}, {"id": "bf3bd6c5-8d61-4064-ac31-288517a963d5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Childhood as social construction", "children": [{"id": "2059479f-c181-41c7-851d-09e75217e03f", "type": "concept", "title": "Protected childhood ideals"}, {"id": "4f9923f9-d8b9-43b0-8be8-1e160b3d9d75", "type": "concept", "title": "Children's labor and responsibility"}, {"id": "f8db9e6a-444f-45a5-a974-ed926a306155", "type": "concept", "title": "Children's agency"}], "description": "Childhood changes across history, culture, class, and institutions."}, {"id": "9de94124-4d77-442e-b650-5cf7fb8fc34c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Development, attachment, and social context", "children": [{"id": "626a01dd-2c95-400a-92c9-54ca1992cd76", "type": "concept", "title": "Attachment and bonding"}, {"id": "7052e971-bf28-43b7-aaff-344d7b23df92", "type": "concept", "title": "Stress, coping, and resilience"}, {"id": "32dd97e3-929c-4192-b065-c277b9c14ec5", "type": "concept", "title": "Adverse childhood experiences"}], "description": "Graduate-level developmental material adds nuance to how early relationships and environments shape children."}]}, {"id": "6715924a-f0eb-4229-9301-96e6942c39ff", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Care Work, and Household Labor", "description": "Family life depends on paid and unpaid labor, much of it gendered and undervalued. Students study how care work organizes inequality inside households and across the economy.", "children": [{"id": "60fa387f-8a9a-4a4c-9a3b-952404380613", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Division of household labor", "children": [{"id": "e0719b62-bc69-4b62-a674-1c0f0f5182af", "type": "concept", "title": "Second shift"}, {"id": "a97e173b-e88b-4f60-91f8-1d92835cf3a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Mental load"}, {"id": "429ce540-7eb0-4bb6-b5f7-7a5cb951b7ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender ideology and bargaining"}], "description": "Students examine how couples and families negotiate chores, childcare, emotion work, and decision-making."}, {"id": "d79ff8a6-cdc1-499b-889b-c3402ec48427", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Care work and social reproduction", "children": [{"id": "d3cb1707-7405-4e73-8e5b-50d0572bb220", "type": "concept", "title": "Unpaid care"}, {"id": "bd91e64d-55e7-40d3-9189-386e33392dd9", "type": "concept", "title": "Paid care workers"}, {"id": "b866e116-821b-4df4-a729-c46e77162c1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Global care chains"}], "description": "Care work sustains people and economies but is often invisible or poorly paid."}, {"id": "4da14784-fd07-4647-a847-c5ce7fd7ed6d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Masculinities, femininities, and family roles", "children": [{"id": "d8a0bc9f-021f-4d1b-ac7d-7c296f798e1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Motherhood penalties"}, {"id": "c0105694-a4ad-4f98-ae42-ebb822bec85c", "type": "concept", "title": "Involved fatherhood"}, {"id": "b6a7801f-5d5c-43b2-98c0-7cffb7980008", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonbinary and gender-diverse family roles"}], "description": "Gender expectations shape fatherhood, motherhood, partnership, and responsibility."}]}, {"id": "3799a5a1-05c7-4160-b470-f1398f862898", "type": "topic", "title": "Work, Class, and Family Inequality", "description": "Families are shaped by jobs, wages, housing, debt, schedules, wealth, and public supports. Students learn how class inequality enters everyday family life.", "children": [{"id": "376c33ad-bd4d-443d-ab10-0cbe1d3c582d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Economic resources and family formation", "children": [{"id": "9a14152f-b751-43ec-908b-17f9e12fdb4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Income and wealth"}, {"id": "14279e6f-a6f2-4b30-9521-f864f34d5bea", "type": "concept", "title": "Employment insecurity"}, {"id": "784db569-b216-4eac-be73-fa93be6f0987", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing affordability"}], "description": "Economic security influences partnership, fertility, marriage timing, and household stability."}, {"id": "47d1726d-59ba-4e4f-9cf7-03a386b241da", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Working families and time pressure", "children": [{"id": "2341bd0d-4195-4396-84b2-a992f4850bd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Shift work and precarious work"}, {"id": "14ef1f6f-8dc0-4985-bdf2-addb53288a15", "type": "concept", "title": "Work-family conflict"}, {"id": "23a696a4-2b81-4518-a74e-6bf60011dcc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Childcare availability"}], "description": "Work schedules structure care, stress, and family time."}, {"id": "615600aa-243c-4d21-bde6-4ae580710110", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Class reproduction", "children": [{"id": "cb2b87de-4212-4166-8af6-bde11562e7d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Intergenerational mobility"}, {"id": "e04f8c5a-88e2-430b-9d8e-7cd3912294fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Educational investment"}, {"id": "fa6c3743-a7fc-4ab7-a9ee-d7350a24dc6a", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural and social capital"}], "description": "Families transmit advantage and disadvantage through money, networks, schools, neighborhoods, and cultural capital."}]}, {"id": "b01e2036-5cef-42c8-82d5-2a4bd7c7e923", "type": "topic", "title": "Race, Ethnicity, Migration, and Family Life", "description": "Family experiences are shaped by racial formation, immigration policy, cultural tradition, discrimination, and unequal access to resources. Students learn to avoid deficit narratives and study family resilience alongside structural constraint.", "children": [{"id": "9993e422-6727-43d9-8428-b54ccf27e518", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race and family inequality", "children": [{"id": "b0290fd6-53f5-403c-bff5-9c5cd159120c", "type": "concept", "title": "Structural racism"}, {"id": "82a7dd19-641f-4006-963d-aa63bb4434d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Segregation and neighborhood effects"}, {"id": "fae85799-1ffc-4433-9a22-962c99aafc89", "type": "concept", "title": "Criminal justice and family disruption"}], "description": "Students examine how racialized institutions shape family formation, wealth, health, housing, and care."}, {"id": "151e7758-0a58-4a48-8539-3a2ba27634b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Immigration and transnational families", "children": [{"id": "b238e34f-768e-4ad9-b361-a15672020e64", "type": "concept", "title": "Transnational parenting"}, {"id": "bd4db37e-e0a9-4b89-982a-1ec0871354f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Remittances"}, {"id": "d9e475b2-85ad-4c6e-a6ee-a71f3fc9bd80", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal status and mixed-status families"}], "description": "Migration can separate, reconnect, and transform families across borders."}, {"id": "37ef6e7b-9480-4f03-8dd9-b536cca59d14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Culture, adaptation, and identity", "children": [{"id": "83c9c814-df6f-44b9-b5ce-d3b24846c616", "type": "concept", "title": "Bicultural family life"}, {"id": "235d18c8-534b-4611-ae0a-83be4ba03bc2", "type": "concept", "title": "Language and intergenerational ties"}, {"id": "bb7f4aaf-2746-4a7e-8b4d-26f64febc598", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion, ritual, and belonging"}], "description": "Culture matters, but students learn to analyze it without stereotyping or ignoring power."}]}, {"id": "6207ded8-baed-4797-94d9-01190c557719", "type": "topic", "title": "LGBTQ+ Families and Queer Kinship", "description": "LGBTQ+ families challenge narrow assumptions about marriage, parenting, gender, kinship, and legitimacy. Students study legal change, everyday family practices, and ongoing inequalities.", "children": [{"id": "3c80de68-b95c-45dd-bb0d-09514a78f8c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Legal recognition and family rights", "children": [{"id": "fda937b8-55ce-4aca-9087-abed2f146b87", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage equality"}, {"id": "94ca8a57-be0b-48ca-9569-254de051838b", "type": "concept", "title": "Adoption and assisted reproduction"}, {"id": "345b7afe-f4bf-4489-bffb-a7803ab94c9e", "type": "concept", "title": "Parental recognition and custody"}], "description": "Students examine how law shapes access to marriage, adoption, custody, healthcare, and benefits."}, {"id": "627bdcc3-952f-4da4-967b-9de889ed823a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Queer kinship and chosen family", "children": [{"id": "1420e5c8-c742-49c7-a817-e92b639de1c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Friendship as kinship"}, {"id": "477167a6-3394-4bda-864b-74b4d036d14d", "type": "concept", "title": "Mutual aid"}, {"id": "73d5c786-f54b-404c-b3ce-d2ef37aa8746", "type": "concept", "title": "Community care in LGBTQ+ history"}], "description": "Chosen family shows that care and obligation often exceed biological or legal ties."}, {"id": "00e7adbe-de41-424d-88ad-6fc03f3278ce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parenting and family diversity", "children": [{"id": "a38eeb81-7793-416b-80d7-39296ca9083e", "type": "concept", "title": "Child outcomes research"}, {"id": "cd89f126-d964-4555-8adc-d562ff91d6f8", "type": "concept", "title": "School and healthcare contexts"}, {"id": "bb208c2c-ef0a-480e-acab-f1b498fb57ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Trans and nonbinary parents"}], "description": "Students review research on LGBTQ+ parenting while examining stigma and institutional barriers."}]}, {"id": "65d14fb1-e21f-4f40-9653-225b8e96fc91", "type": "topic", "title": "Single-Parent, Extended, and Multigenerational Families", "description": "Households vary widely, and many families rely on extended kin, single parents, grandparents, and shared caregiving. Students learn to interpret these forms sociologically rather than through deficit assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "d64d4cee-e5b0-457f-a2a4-2b79ad3a55dc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Single-parent families", "children": [{"id": "1eccba15-1c55-47de-b105-10f6732ab56b", "type": "concept", "title": "Never-married, divorced, and widowed parents"}, {"id": "d4e6352f-f23f-453a-b2c9-91fbe77a5dac", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic vulnerability"}, {"id": "88faac9f-d2d6-4703-9dff-58255c044b16", "type": "concept", "title": "Support networks and resilience"}], "description": "Students examine diversity among single-parent households and the role of policy and resources."}, {"id": "f5f6f790-78bc-45d3-bc9b-c3eb36949359", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Extended and multigenerational households", "children": [{"id": "48e0db63-a74f-4c53-bfff-2c4d2e316b9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Grandparent caregiving"}, {"id": "88a07707-93d2-4546-9d35-3e09b652be3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Adult children at home"}, {"id": "02a85490-b372-4493-b64f-0a3424b5a480", "type": "concept", "title": "Shared housing and kin networks"}], "description": "Extended households can reflect culture, economic strategy, migration, housing pressure, or caregiving needs."}, {"id": "12c89e23-1b5e-424f-af6e-24dee5b2283d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Chosen and fictive kin", "children": [{"id": "39194092-b014-4af2-b196-ff9ccc41a4b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Godparents and community kin"}, {"id": "a61287aa-7ce0-4ee1-b5bc-577776202a09", "type": "concept", "title": "Friendship networks"}, {"id": "e47d9c02-e33c-40bc-9f4b-70f327674a54", "type": "concept", "title": "Mutual aid and care circles"}], "description": "Nontraditional kinship networks provide emotional, practical, and survival support."}]}, {"id": "87546bcd-d1c4-4c34-b034-b43ceaeeaf07", "type": "topic", "title": "Family Violence, Trauma, and Safety", "description": "Family can be a source of care and harm. This topic treats violence and trauma with analytic seriousness, connecting individual experiences to gender, power, inequality, law, and social services.", "children": [{"id": "fe9fe874-745b-46ef-97b1-44b97f88f7e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intimate partner violence", "children": [{"id": "ec28c383-ce96-4a9a-b836-7a58f89ea129", "type": "concept", "title": "Coercive control"}, {"id": "59ba2e41-6909-4876-b6f1-5450c86bd67b", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and power"}, {"id": "480c74cd-57e7-46f3-8181-04d44ef2ab98", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal and service systems"}], "description": "Students study patterns of coercive control, violence, barriers to leaving, and institutional response."}, {"id": "a79557c8-ddd0-472a-90b0-85b0d2042195", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Child maltreatment and adverse experiences", "children": [{"id": "5d54d60c-577a-473e-9a10-1b9939639e11", "type": "concept", "title": "Neglect and abuse categories"}, {"id": "ed3b0463-acd8-49bd-b605-acc1d24a76d4", "type": "concept", "title": "ACEs research"}, {"id": "b0224f56-e080-4614-bc49-ef4ce5e5c14a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mandatory reporting and its tensions"}], "description": "Students connect child safety to family stress, resources, trauma, and prevention."}, {"id": "730be424-c6f8-4946-9dfb-6716c5185eff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Trauma-informed and structural perspectives", "children": [{"id": "4cc7b9dc-8105-44f0-8395-edae1e7ebcdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Poverty, housing, and stress"}, {"id": "c6bfe279-b127-4946-9777-853201b0a89f", "type": "concept", "title": "Community violence"}, {"id": "a98deda7-f21f-427e-9d93-301fb9894c3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Prevention and survivor-centered policy"}], "description": "A sociological lens avoids reducing violence to individual pathology alone."}]}, {"id": "fd60217b-06dd-4842-8431-b9dc042fb2d7", "type": "topic", "title": "Family Policy, Law, and the Welfare State", "description": "Families are governed by policies around marriage, taxation, childcare, leave, welfare, immigration, healthcare, education, and eldercare. Students learn how states define and support families unevenly.", "children": [{"id": "1036e108-11ea-4d10-ae30-7b716923423e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Family law and state recognition", "children": [{"id": "8b02b2b7-a0ec-4228-8117-b27484a958e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage and divorce law"}, {"id": "1dc348fc-64d7-4397-bd7a-90c8347788a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Custody and child support"}, {"id": "977a16f9-b726-4385-8741-7ddc84e216b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Adoption and reproductive law"}], "description": "Law determines whose relationships count and what obligations are enforceable."}, {"id": "3da7bf6c-62ca-437c-81a9-74cd8e92b1ed", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Welfare-state supports", "children": [{"id": "79e4f5ba-6e20-4bdc-8b69-5e6577a8e53a", "type": "concept", "title": "Paid family leave"}, {"id": "6319129d-eea9-421a-9c8a-7397a560b063", "type": "concept", "title": "Childcare and child allowances"}, {"id": "f053202b-22fa-4ad8-8652-3535ed998aa9", "type": "concept", "title": "Healthcare, housing, and food assistance"}], "description": "Students compare policies that support or penalize caregiving."}, {"id": "206ebb39-6ba5-4718-9818-57f0d552b62e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Policy debates and unintended consequences", "children": [{"id": "dab07e42-ccbe-42e9-81cb-28855cc8e43b", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage promotion"}, {"id": "5491439a-e38c-4db5-b967-bf0d15695bb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Work requirements"}, {"id": "dc8b5dcf-b9de-4b02-ad1b-c2a9a292b40f", "type": "concept", "title": "Universal versus targeted benefits"}], "description": "Family policy often reflects moral assumptions and political conflict."}]}, {"id": "23fd45ee-b785-4523-84ae-427bfee3977f", "type": "topic", "title": "Cross-Cultural and Global Family Variation", "description": "Family forms vary across societies, and globalization intensifies comparison, migration, and policy learning. Students study family diversity without ranking cultures on a simplistic scale.", "children": [{"id": "944c3522-ca41-489d-ab44-c0ccf42ac112", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative family systems", "children": [{"id": "3a39b749-8da8-466a-8064-4c2f264a34c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Patrilineal and matrilineal systems"}, {"id": "c317a28d-b870-4ebb-8943-71307aa2d56c", "type": "concept", "title": "Arranged and love marriage"}, {"id": "614cd96a-495a-4d97-97ee-742b61575e42", "type": "concept", "title": "Extended kin obligations"}], "description": "Students compare marriage, kinship, household, inheritance, and caregiving across contexts."}, {"id": "6afaf4eb-736a-4ff3-927e-9700eb00f8d3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Globalization and family change", "children": [{"id": "6c33355e-779d-427d-9e57-c9bb93ac9a22", "type": "concept", "title": "Global care chains"}, {"id": "dbfc3777-7df0-4941-8e5a-0a8845acf5bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora households"}, {"id": "699bf3f6-8228-4a93-9452-ce3fb702b24f", "type": "concept", "title": "Transnational adoption and reproduction"}], "description": "Economic integration, migration, media, and conflict reshape family life worldwide."}, {"id": "998fe9be-7a1b-4d2a-b990-59260b843eda", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Human rights and family policy", "children": [{"id": "335a08f5-117a-446e-87f7-9b0b3e3a549a", "type": "concept", "title": "UN family and child frameworks"}, {"id": "a0667f38-4b11-4587-96ef-452a4051757f", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender policy comparisons"}, {"id": "f28d0d79-4b7c-48e0-b3ab-53dc7ef93c4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Care infrastructure across welfare regimes"}], "description": "Global family debates often involve gender equality, children's rights, eldercare, and migration."}]}, {"id": "0a054ddc-9c6e-42a5-b6de-d76fc58f1ab4", "type": "topic", "title": "Aging, Intergenerational Relations, and Caregiving", "description": "Longer life expectancy and population aging transform family obligations. Students examine eldercare, grandparenting, inheritance, intergenerational solidarity, and conflict.", "children": [{"id": "ae0d3c4e-37d6-47e5-8240-6f432fdac08c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intergenerational ties", "children": [{"id": "37069da5-4d0c-4dee-91a6-95fdbc700d4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Intergenerational solidarity"}, {"id": "79746e02-5bbb-4a0e-8ecb-8c32cda49253", "type": "concept", "title": "Family conflict"}, {"id": "7fe98808-2b93-4f2f-a75c-59ddb563e1fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Inheritance and wealth transfer"}], "description": "Family relationships unfold across the life course and link generations through care, money, memory, and obligation."}, {"id": "b802a994-42ea-4fc9-ae9b-0267128911bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Aging and eldercare", "children": [{"id": "05a5d851-1d90-4a8d-806d-000e3cbc7b4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Informal caregiving"}, {"id": "4024a2e3-bfc6-4fde-8596-5a0e9f62de66", "type": "concept", "title": "Long-term care systems"}, {"id": "86bafcd5-9d5d-4898-92b1-915555851261", "type": "concept", "title": "Sandwich generation"}], "description": "Students study how families manage care needs amid unequal resources and limited public supports."}, {"id": "b1e1b827-84e2-4103-86ee-cbdc21e0c084", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Grandparents and kin care", "children": [{"id": "aba31dad-82a8-4975-a23f-bfa13816c626", "type": "concept", "title": "Grandfamilies"}, {"id": "c2b30bbe-4ee2-45c2-827b-79be9866a5fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Custodial grandparenting"}, {"id": "a09946c7-fbe4-4df0-b839-3da1c1e92c19", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural transmission"}], "description": "Grandparents often play central roles in childcare, housing, culture, and family stability."}]}, {"id": "a195ee15-3d1a-4725-990c-d5569ccd8a8a", "type": "topic", "title": "Technology, Media, and Reproductive Futures", "description": "Technology changes dating, fertility, parenting, surveillance, work-family boundaries, and kinship. Students learn to analyze new tools as social arrangements rather than neutral inventions.", "children": [{"id": "5362cff9-43be-48a2-ba67-ca1c95e558ff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital family life", "children": [{"id": "8a5c41b1-01bf-47de-8b88-5b5bf1145b8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Family group chats"}, {"id": "5cf089fc-6316-4ccc-b735-aaeb6be325be", "type": "concept", "title": "Parenting and screen time"}, {"id": "4d808360-b801-4e12-b9cc-5b59cffd84de", "type": "concept", "title": "Surveillance and privacy"}], "description": "Phones, platforms, and media reshape intimacy, conflict, care, and family communication."}, {"id": "fb7498c4-3523-4790-9418-779922fbf1f2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Assisted reproduction and kinship", "children": [{"id": "ddd535a2-9200-4ace-8631-fdf708cbb213", "type": "concept", "title": "IVF and donor conception"}, {"id": "25be5028-d763-4d9e-9321-074f37f033d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Surrogacy"}, {"id": "c4f9ba4a-2626-4882-8239-e91671757d25", "type": "concept", "title": "Genetic testing and ancestry platforms"}], "description": "Reproductive technologies raise questions about biology, law, markets, inequality, and parenthood."}, {"id": "28dcd7de-a147-467f-99bf-b3f048602c8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Future family debates", "children": [{"id": "38632fd2-c240-4acb-bd40-d5b40b25efe0", "type": "concept", "title": "Remote work and family life"}, {"id": "53a99f92-ced7-4ce6-a49c-e82e6fe32225", "type": "concept", "title": "AI companionship and care technologies"}, {"id": "c90eb271-e82d-49dd-88a8-35d30ca9e15a", "type": "concept", "title": "Bioethics and access inequality"}], "description": "Students evaluate emerging issues without treating novelty as destiny."}]}, {"id": "77a62fd3-cfc0-4215-8f2c-b5bc0bd6a8e8", "type": "topic", "title": "Family Research, Public Scholarship, and Final Synthesis", "description": "The course ends by teaching students to use family research responsibly in public debate. Students synthesize evidence, theory, policy, and lived experience into a rigorous final project.", "children": [{"id": "32a0ee48-4065-48aa-9d2c-f5a4b5e30187", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evaluating public claims about family", "children": [{"id": "782a9414-c7bf-439e-88e3-89ad8e3e445c", "type": "concept", "title": "Media literacy"}, {"id": "744b3c77-2419-450a-9c6a-ad1347c4e30c", "type": "concept", "title": "Research briefs"}, {"id": "34136971-5802-4400-9e59-2d3cb7ff55fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence versus anecdote"}], "description": "Students learn to identify moral panic, weak evidence, and misleading causal claims."}, {"id": "a6310eda-7875-4da1-9540-1b8486b794b0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Designing a family sociology project", "children": [{"id": "31594343-4fc8-4752-b463-4eb91b0cd0f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question and literature review"}, {"id": "919f670c-da85-4648-9b2b-4a3bc405738d", "type": "concept", "title": "Data source selection"}, {"id": "862b4c17-1d05-43ce-b631-15c96cab069c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics and positionality"}], "description": "Students translate a family issue into a researchable question and appropriate evidence."}, {"id": "802d8efe-301c-435c-926c-71226e7a63ec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Final synthesis and communication", "children": [{"id": "b73b2980-8377-4eca-a205-34e0f5495104", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy memo"}, {"id": "eb9c998f-aab5-4a6f-bbdc-1f69f84c9f42", "type": "concept", "title": "Public-facing explainer"}, {"id": "66494004-fe11-440e-a36a-722cf6ad5cda", "type": "concept", "title": "Final research presentation"}], "description": "Students practice communicating family sociology to scholarly and public audiences."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$sofamjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'sociology-of-the-family'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Sociology of the Family',
  goal = 'Sociology of the Family',
  summary = 'This course frames family as a social institution, intimate relationship system, legal category, cultural ideal, and site of inequality. Definitions of family shape law, benefits, identity, obligation, and belonging, and family forms have always changed even when public debate imagines a stable traditional past. The focus runs through data literacy, intimacy and marriage, divorce and repartnering, parenting, gendered care work, class, race and migration, LGBTQ+ kinship, varied households, violence and safety, family policy, cross-cultural variation, aging and caregiving, technology and reproductive futures, and using family research responsibly in public debate.',
  data = $sofamjson${"slug": "sociology-of-the-family", "title": "Sociology of the Family", "description": "This course frames family as a social institution, intimate relationship system, legal category, cultural ideal, and site of inequality. Definitions of family shape law, benefits, identity, obligation, and belonging, and family forms have always changed even when public debate imagines a stable traditional past. The focus runs through data literacy, intimacy and marriage, divorce and repartnering, parenting, gendered care work, class, race and migration, LGBTQ+ kinship, varied households, violence and safety, family policy, cross-cultural variation, aging and caregiving, technology and reproductive futures, and using family research responsibly in public debate.", "topics": [{"id": "11a192f4-e2bd-4674-86ea-a2e34f7472be", "type": "topic", "title": "The Sociological Study of Family", "description": "This opening topic frames family as a social institution, intimate relationship system, legal category, cultural ideal, and site of inequality. Students learn to move beyond personal experience toward evidence-based sociological analysis.", "children": [{"id": "563527ee-c7eb-4c96-9162-a333861010fc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Family as institution and lived experience", "children": [{"id": "93374f39-87ce-464a-8b3e-f336ea0338a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Private troubles and public issues"}, {"id": "ffbbdf8a-0671-4c0e-9bdc-1cafb625d4e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Family as social institution"}, {"id": "09aaf51e-760e-4433-9e0d-4b95e6092ba7", "type": "concept", "title": "Micro, meso, and macro levels"}], "description": "Students learn why family is both deeply personal and publicly organized by law, labor markets, culture, and policy."}, {"id": "da7b3905-82b0-435f-a08d-3135144f4e0e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Major theoretical lenses", "children": [{"id": "5512dad5-4ee7-4cdf-ac73-f423f437fb22", "type": "concept", "title": "Functionalism and social reproduction"}, {"id": "b712cff6-2897-43ba-aceb-2e256e40bfa9", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict and feminist theories"}, {"id": "ba7df8ec-e4f6-48d9-9948-9473c092a086", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbolic interactionism and life-course theory"}], "description": "Theory gives students competing ways to explain family stability, conflict, meaning, and change."}, {"id": "7b86d74c-1702-4d00-8890-8d98b67d2be8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence and family research", "children": [{"id": "d9ecdc98-ada3-44c7-bc5f-d4519bf06eee", "type": "concept", "title": "Quantitative family demography"}, {"id": "4ec573bd-15ec-49f6-a745-426eab83b482", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualitative family narratives"}, {"id": "eb8655c4-69b3-4f09-8b4d-d9354e9eef3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics and sensitive topics"}], "description": "Students learn how sociologists study families through surveys, interviews, ethnography, administrative data, and historical evidence."}]}, {"id": "db413992-3606-453d-8ea1-ef9067ecf8b5", "type": "topic", "title": "Defining Family, Kinship, and Household", "description": "Definitions of family shape law, benefits, identity, obligation, and belonging. This topic helps students see family as a contested category rather than a single natural form.", "children": [{"id": "8eed1fc7-27eb-4962-b220-844e89cb8222", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Family, household, and kinship", "children": [{"id": "6b19948e-c38b-4272-ae00-7d3d07def94a", "type": "concept", "title": "Biological, legal, and social kin"}, {"id": "2594059c-d0bd-43a0-9f2f-574cb117dc09", "type": "concept", "title": "Households and co-residence"}, {"id": "72d9acb3-7bd7-4b50-933d-0fcffb5db7bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Chosen and fictive kin"}], "description": "Students distinguish related but different concepts that are often collapsed in everyday speech."}, {"id": "f6f188c1-8668-42c7-9c26-de2f5c9c56fe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Norms and boundary-making", "children": [{"id": "62d1a587-684f-4768-98d2-35f18ffdeab4", "type": "concept", "title": "Nuclear-family ideal"}, {"id": "5c55e1c7-6b3b-41ce-b996-4d863266aafa", "type": "concept", "title": "Legitimacy and stigma"}, {"id": "7431cb18-f932-4348-9646-9e3d323d96e3", "type": "concept", "title": "State and institutional classification"}], "description": "Definitions include some relationships while excluding others from recognition and resources."}, {"id": "b8ffbdd5-b9db-4ca3-ad9e-24e6684d887f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Measuring family", "children": [{"id": "e56c4b3f-26ea-43bd-9a25-19062ec32ef2", "type": "concept", "title": "Household surveys"}, {"id": "912460b2-fb57-4146-88ae-3a8980014017", "type": "concept", "title": "Relationship categories"}, {"id": "4c92a5ea-43b9-413c-9417-bca8974aac72", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of official data"}], "description": "Students learn how categories used in surveys and censuses shape what researchers can see."}]}, {"id": "f7e3dd3d-76ce-4dd4-9d6a-b174c5a90edf", "type": "topic", "title": "Historical Change and Family Myths", "description": "Family forms have always changed, but public debate often imagines a stable traditional past. Students learn to evaluate nostalgia, myth, and evidence about family change.", "children": [{"id": "ad53b633-3653-4b22-ad6a-0ee559d1dfbb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historical diversity of family forms", "children": [{"id": "597d94ec-65c2-4228-83ad-871f6c75e3d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Extended households"}, {"id": "61e971a9-827b-4066-8074-bda1c15a6897", "type": "concept", "title": "Apprenticeship, servitude, and domestic labor"}, {"id": "839e83aa-f887-487b-8e70-4e8d26f7a333", "type": "concept", "title": "Industrialization and separate spheres"}], "description": "Students examine how family structures shifted with economy, religion, law, migration, and demographic change."}, {"id": "a2fafac9-b3ce-45ee-9500-d5b22415941c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The traditional family myth", "children": [{"id": "1c0f6256-c314-44ac-b565-103449845edb", "type": "concept", "title": "The 1950s family ideal"}, {"id": "605c6f89-39bf-495c-9253-ff47337d3004", "type": "concept", "title": "Stephanie Coontz and family nostalgia"}, {"id": "b42e422d-b8fc-430a-b562-1ec5f39123c0", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, class, and exclusion in family ideals"}], "description": "Historical evidence complicates claims that one family model is timeless or universal."}, {"id": "4c4e4b92-9c7d-487e-b259-e02c028ccd69", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modernization and demographic transition", "children": [{"id": "7264f8ee-04d4-4cc1-831e-d98a520a97ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Declining fertility"}, {"id": "06a025fe-d394-422c-a19c-1e9eb38c8d29", "type": "concept", "title": "Rising life expectancy"}, {"id": "a7214693-1904-4822-823a-11613c52cafe", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage timing and household size"}], "description": "Students connect fertility, mortality, education, and labor-market change to family transformation."}]}, {"id": "48d1d99a-a750-40c9-ac0c-25b9a45da22a", "type": "topic", "title": "Family Demography and Data Literacy", "description": "Family sociology depends on careful use of data. Students learn to interpret trends in marriage, divorce, fertility, cohabitation, household composition, and child wellbeing without overstating what the numbers prove.", "children": [{"id": "34d2b110-f291-4a21-8c9b-d2f28acb13e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Core demographic indicators", "children": [{"id": "05eca7a0-af6a-4173-8672-907bceb27443", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage and divorce rates"}, {"id": "aa01a40c-e0b2-40a9-b28f-826025d6f30c", "type": "concept", "title": "Fertility and birth timing"}, {"id": "b63a1e37-a2dc-4e59-80a5-6cf4e3ab1bc3", "type": "concept", "title": "Household and living-arrangement measures"}], "description": "Indicators help students compare family change across time, place, and social groups."}, {"id": "a7de0978-a427-487a-839f-924d1ff39a71", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Data sources and limitations", "children": [{"id": "31374301-070d-4072-91be-0e2ed64fc6a8", "type": "concept", "title": "Census and American Community Survey"}, {"id": "3e6e4138-53ea-4396-9b29-441345fd6f73", "type": "concept", "title": "Longitudinal surveys"}, {"id": "588fc20e-e813-485a-b0e1-c402d28cc48b", "type": "concept", "title": "Administrative and qualitative data"}], "description": "Students learn where family data comes from and what each source misses."}, {"id": "0a875116-5e46-4481-8de2-15b4b0f7900b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading family statistics critically", "children": [{"id": "1c6935ce-3b67-4e0b-b17c-dceba4d7241b", "type": "concept", "title": "Rates versus counts"}, {"id": "69c48da3-3312-4441-b138-fc64852d1a94", "type": "concept", "title": "Selection effects"}, {"id": "3193802f-aaf4-4514-8965-051b376fb8ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Correlation and causation"}], "description": "Data literacy prevents moral panic and simplistic causal claims."}]}, {"id": "ea62c89a-56e0-4e46-b6ba-0c008ae86324", "type": "topic", "title": "Intimacy, Dating, Cohabitation, and Marriage", "description": "Intimate relationships are shaped by emotion, culture, technology, economics, gender expectations, and law. Students study marriage and partnership as social arrangements that change over time.", "children": [{"id": "9afadc49-bb3d-42b1-90d6-9b443910e6e4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dating and partner selection", "children": [{"id": "70bae7f5-c1f9-426b-8f62-28399eaf164a", "type": "concept", "title": "Assortative mating"}, {"id": "bf9787f0-7477-4fb9-a33e-edf45459f252", "type": "concept", "title": "Online dating and algorithms"}, {"id": "0c2be20b-137c-41c9-a58a-76d504a115d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Race, class, religion, and education in partner choice"}], "description": "Students examine how intimacy is patterned by social networks, markets, norms, and inequality."}, {"id": "1b7c1ea2-7ace-41f4-8a52-8956e0faa76a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cohabitation and relationship sequencing", "children": [{"id": "76c74157-bda1-4e5f-b8de-154c9f81fd74", "type": "concept", "title": "Cohabitation as trial marriage"}, {"id": "815ea71d-c259-43a7-97c7-3e422e5cbd0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic constraints"}, {"id": "63933500-3c25-463c-8421-9d2d29507364", "type": "concept", "title": "Relationship progression and ambiguity"}], "description": "Cohabitation has become a major relationship form with different meanings across groups."}, {"id": "778928d4-64ab-4f0f-9709-1e43450f2e2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Marriage as institution", "children": [{"id": "358d47ce-78a6-47ae-93a5-c39604a316c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal rights and obligations"}, {"id": "bddb5a0a-f35f-4dfe-8adc-0a4a9bca0bc4", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage markets"}, {"id": "e15f951c-7cab-446f-bca9-e64c3f30192b", "type": "concept", "title": "Changing marriage expectations"}], "description": "Marriage links intimacy to law, property, status, benefits, and cultural recognition."}]}, {"id": "a5b1b0e3-2abb-4c37-aff5-8c586e23ed63", "type": "topic", "title": "Divorce, Separation, Repartnering, and Stepfamilies", "description": "Divorce and repartnering reshape kinship, parenting, economics, and emotional life. Students learn to analyze separation without moral panic or simplistic assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "a087b109-8e91-4853-a460-7fe049158587", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Divorce trends and causes", "children": [{"id": "ee34d038-b08c-456e-bfcc-42ea65986e3f", "type": "concept", "title": "No-fault divorce"}, {"id": "3c536306-a615-45c0-87b8-d23f76db8578", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic strain"}, {"id": "1e424365-b399-4efc-9c71-df5119b3f615", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender roles and relationship quality"}], "description": "Students study divorce as a patterned social process influenced by economics, gender, culture, law, and expectations."}, {"id": "688d2494-0b9d-410d-b464-0b02fa0107cf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Consequences for adults and children", "children": [{"id": "69aa646b-190b-4357-a812-5770890d77bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic consequences"}, {"id": "899a3f0a-d553-4d7d-bdd7-4fc6557d753e", "type": "concept", "title": "Child adjustment debates"}, {"id": "5159e68b-cb6d-4149-bb8a-86f66e864f30", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflict versus divorce effects"}], "description": "Outcomes depend on conflict, resources, stability, parenting, and support systems."}, {"id": "0c0d1464-3a53-457e-8924-7da936a58b98", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Repartnering and stepfamilies", "children": [{"id": "3b02b704-a012-404f-b710-df09a4996148", "type": "concept", "title": "Blended families"}, {"id": "d60747bf-70ae-4006-b3ca-22eb20ae15af", "type": "concept", "title": "Co-parenting across households"}, {"id": "fa565a90-25e3-4f30-8c1a-9e630659480c", "type": "concept", "title": "Step-parent roles and boundaries"}], "description": "New partnerships create complex kinship obligations and household forms."}]}, {"id": "acdf5c90-5ad4-4fb4-9a5d-8053e8619508", "type": "topic", "title": "Parenting, Childhood, and Socialization", "description": "Families are central sites of socialization, caregiving, discipline, identity formation, and opportunity. Students connect parenting practices to culture, class, policy, and child development.", "children": [{"id": "b8188528-7830-46de-8b27-de1ad375d711", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parenting styles and practices", "children": [{"id": "909267f3-c45e-4b5b-b956-b5a8f5a1b6e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Authoritative, authoritarian, permissive, and neglectful models"}, {"id": "61a70905-4701-47cf-a4e0-d716735e8753", "type": "concept", "title": "Concerted cultivation and natural growth"}, {"id": "78b7c476-8d7a-4bed-9504-2b31d359e4ef", "type": "concept", "title": "Intensive parenting"}], "description": "Students learn that parenting norms are culturally and historically situated."}, {"id": "bf3bd6c5-8d61-4064-ac31-288517a963d5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Childhood as social construction", "children": [{"id": "2059479f-c181-41c7-851d-09e75217e03f", "type": "concept", "title": "Protected childhood ideals"}, {"id": "4f9923f9-d8b9-43b0-8be8-1e160b3d9d75", "type": "concept", "title": "Children's labor and responsibility"}, {"id": "f8db9e6a-444f-45a5-a974-ed926a306155", "type": "concept", "title": "Children's agency"}], "description": "Childhood changes across history, culture, class, and institutions."}, {"id": "9de94124-4d77-442e-b650-5cf7fb8fc34c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Development, attachment, and social context", "children": [{"id": "626a01dd-2c95-400a-92c9-54ca1992cd76", "type": "concept", "title": "Attachment and bonding"}, {"id": "7052e971-bf28-43b7-aaff-344d7b23df92", "type": "concept", "title": "Stress, coping, and resilience"}, {"id": "32dd97e3-929c-4192-b065-c277b9c14ec5", "type": "concept", "title": "Adverse childhood experiences"}], "description": "Graduate-level developmental material adds nuance to how early relationships and environments shape children."}]}, {"id": "6715924a-f0eb-4229-9301-96e6942c39ff", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Care Work, and Household Labor", "description": "Family life depends on paid and unpaid labor, much of it gendered and undervalued. Students study how care work organizes inequality inside households and across the economy.", "children": [{"id": "60fa387f-8a9a-4a4c-9a3b-952404380613", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Division of household labor", "children": [{"id": "e0719b62-bc69-4b62-a674-1c0f0f5182af", "type": "concept", "title": "Second shift"}, {"id": "a97e173b-e88b-4f60-91f8-1d92835cf3a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Mental load"}, {"id": "429ce540-7eb0-4bb6-b5f7-7a5cb951b7ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender ideology and bargaining"}], "description": "Students examine how couples and families negotiate chores, childcare, emotion work, and decision-making."}, {"id": "d79ff8a6-cdc1-499b-889b-c3402ec48427", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Care work and social reproduction", "children": [{"id": "d3cb1707-7405-4e73-8e5b-50d0572bb220", "type": "concept", "title": "Unpaid care"}, {"id": "bd91e64d-55e7-40d3-9189-386e33392dd9", "type": "concept", "title": "Paid care workers"}, {"id": "b866e116-821b-4df4-a729-c46e77162c1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Global care chains"}], "description": "Care work sustains people and economies but is often invisible or poorly paid."}, {"id": "4da14784-fd07-4647-a847-c5ce7fd7ed6d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Masculinities, femininities, and family roles", "children": [{"id": "d8a0bc9f-021f-4d1b-ac7d-7c296f798e1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Motherhood penalties"}, {"id": "c0105694-a4ad-4f98-ae42-ebb822bec85c", "type": "concept", "title": "Involved fatherhood"}, {"id": "b6a7801f-5d5c-43b2-98c0-7cffb7980008", "type": "concept", "title": "Nonbinary and gender-diverse family roles"}], "description": "Gender expectations shape fatherhood, motherhood, partnership, and responsibility."}]}, {"id": "3799a5a1-05c7-4160-b470-f1398f862898", "type": "topic", "title": "Work, Class, and Family Inequality", "description": "Families are shaped by jobs, wages, housing, debt, schedules, wealth, and public supports. Students learn how class inequality enters everyday family life.", "children": [{"id": "376c33ad-bd4d-443d-ab10-0cbe1d3c582d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Economic resources and family formation", "children": [{"id": "9a14152f-b751-43ec-908b-17f9e12fdb4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Income and wealth"}, {"id": "14279e6f-a6f2-4b30-9521-f864f34d5bea", "type": "concept", "title": "Employment insecurity"}, {"id": "784db569-b216-4eac-be73-fa93be6f0987", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing affordability"}], "description": "Economic security influences partnership, fertility, marriage timing, and household stability."}, {"id": "47d1726d-59ba-4e4f-9cf7-03a386b241da", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Working families and time pressure", "children": [{"id": "2341bd0d-4195-4396-84b2-a992f4850bd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Shift work and precarious work"}, {"id": "14ef1f6f-8dc0-4985-bdf2-addb53288a15", "type": "concept", "title": "Work-family conflict"}, {"id": "23a696a4-2b81-4518-a74e-6bf60011dcc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Childcare availability"}], "description": "Work schedules structure care, stress, and family time."}, {"id": "615600aa-243c-4d21-bde6-4ae580710110", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Class reproduction", "children": [{"id": "cb2b87de-4212-4166-8af6-bde11562e7d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Intergenerational mobility"}, {"id": "e04f8c5a-88e2-430b-9d8e-7cd3912294fd", "type": "concept", "title": "Educational investment"}, {"id": "fa6c3743-a7fc-4ab7-a9ee-d7350a24dc6a", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural and social capital"}], "description": "Families transmit advantage and disadvantage through money, networks, schools, neighborhoods, and cultural capital."}]}, {"id": "b01e2036-5cef-42c8-82d5-2a4bd7c7e923", "type": "topic", "title": "Race, Ethnicity, Migration, and Family Life", "description": "Family experiences are shaped by racial formation, immigration policy, cultural tradition, discrimination, and unequal access to resources. Students learn to avoid deficit narratives and study family resilience alongside structural constraint.", "children": [{"id": "9993e422-6727-43d9-8428-b54ccf27e518", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race and family inequality", "children": [{"id": "b0290fd6-53f5-403c-bff5-9c5cd159120c", "type": "concept", "title": "Structural racism"}, {"id": "82a7dd19-641f-4006-963d-aa63bb4434d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Segregation and neighborhood effects"}, {"id": "fae85799-1ffc-4433-9a22-962c99aafc89", "type": "concept", "title": "Criminal justice and family disruption"}], "description": "Students examine how racialized institutions shape family formation, wealth, health, housing, and care."}, {"id": "151e7758-0a58-4a48-8539-3a2ba27634b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Immigration and transnational families", "children": [{"id": "b238e34f-768e-4ad9-b361-a15672020e64", "type": "concept", "title": "Transnational parenting"}, {"id": "bd4db37e-e0a9-4b89-982a-1ec0871354f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Remittances"}, {"id": "d9e475b2-85ad-4c6e-a6ee-a71f3fc9bd80", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal status and mixed-status families"}], "description": "Migration can separate, reconnect, and transform families across borders."}, {"id": "37ef6e7b-9480-4f03-8dd9-b536cca59d14", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Culture, adaptation, and identity", "children": [{"id": "83c9c814-df6f-44b9-b5ce-d3b24846c616", "type": "concept", "title": "Bicultural family life"}, {"id": "235d18c8-534b-4611-ae0a-83be4ba03bc2", "type": "concept", "title": "Language and intergenerational ties"}, {"id": "bb7f4aaf-2746-4a7e-8b4d-26f64febc598", "type": "concept", "title": "Religion, ritual, and belonging"}], "description": "Culture matters, but students learn to analyze it without stereotyping or ignoring power."}]}, {"id": "6207ded8-baed-4797-94d9-01190c557719", "type": "topic", "title": "LGBTQ+ Families and Queer Kinship", "description": "LGBTQ+ families challenge narrow assumptions about marriage, parenting, gender, kinship, and legitimacy. Students study legal change, everyday family practices, and ongoing inequalities.", "children": [{"id": "3c80de68-b95c-45dd-bb0d-09514a78f8c9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Legal recognition and family rights", "children": [{"id": "fda937b8-55ce-4aca-9087-abed2f146b87", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage equality"}, {"id": "94ca8a57-be0b-48ca-9569-254de051838b", "type": "concept", "title": "Adoption and assisted reproduction"}, {"id": "345b7afe-f4bf-4489-bffb-a7803ab94c9e", "type": "concept", "title": "Parental recognition and custody"}], "description": "Students examine how law shapes access to marriage, adoption, custody, healthcare, and benefits."}, {"id": "627bdcc3-952f-4da4-967b-9de889ed823a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Queer kinship and chosen family", "children": [{"id": "1420e5c8-c742-49c7-a817-e92b639de1c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Friendship as kinship"}, {"id": "477167a6-3394-4bda-864b-74b4d036d14d", "type": "concept", "title": "Mutual aid"}, {"id": "73d5c786-f54b-404c-b3ce-d2ef37aa8746", "type": "concept", "title": "Community care in LGBTQ+ history"}], "description": "Chosen family shows that care and obligation often exceed biological or legal ties."}, {"id": "00e7adbe-de41-424d-88ad-6fc03f3278ce", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parenting and family diversity", "children": [{"id": "a38eeb81-7793-416b-80d7-39296ca9083e", "type": "concept", "title": "Child outcomes research"}, {"id": "cd89f126-d964-4555-8adc-d562ff91d6f8", "type": "concept", "title": "School and healthcare contexts"}, {"id": "bb208c2c-ef0a-480e-acab-f1b498fb57ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Trans and nonbinary parents"}], "description": "Students review research on LGBTQ+ parenting while examining stigma and institutional barriers."}]}, {"id": "65d14fb1-e21f-4f40-9653-225b8e96fc91", "type": "topic", "title": "Single-Parent, Extended, and Multigenerational Families", "description": "Households vary widely, and many families rely on extended kin, single parents, grandparents, and shared caregiving. Students learn to interpret these forms sociologically rather than through deficit assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "d64d4cee-e5b0-457f-a2a4-2b79ad3a55dc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Single-parent families", "children": [{"id": "1eccba15-1c55-47de-b105-10f6732ab56b", "type": "concept", "title": "Never-married, divorced, and widowed parents"}, {"id": "d4e6352f-f23f-453a-b2c9-91fbe77a5dac", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic vulnerability"}, {"id": "88faac9f-d2d6-4703-9dff-58255c044b16", "type": "concept", "title": "Support networks and resilience"}], "description": "Students examine diversity among single-parent households and the role of policy and resources."}, {"id": "f5f6f790-78bc-45d3-bc9b-c3eb36949359", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Extended and multigenerational households", "children": [{"id": "48e0db63-a74f-4c53-bfff-2c4d2e316b9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Grandparent caregiving"}, {"id": "88a07707-93d2-4546-9d35-3e09b652be3f", "type": "concept", "title": "Adult children at home"}, {"id": "02a85490-b372-4493-b64f-0a3424b5a480", "type": "concept", "title": "Shared housing and kin networks"}], "description": "Extended households can reflect culture, economic strategy, migration, housing pressure, or caregiving needs."}, {"id": "12c89e23-1b5e-424f-af6e-24dee5b2283d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Chosen and fictive kin", "children": [{"id": "39194092-b014-4af2-b196-ff9ccc41a4b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Godparents and community kin"}, {"id": "a61287aa-7ce0-4ee1-b5bc-577776202a09", "type": "concept", "title": "Friendship networks"}, {"id": "e47d9c02-e33c-40bc-9f4b-70f327674a54", "type": "concept", "title": "Mutual aid and care circles"}], "description": "Nontraditional kinship networks provide emotional, practical, and survival support."}]}, {"id": "87546bcd-d1c4-4c34-b034-b43ceaeeaf07", "type": "topic", "title": "Family Violence, Trauma, and Safety", "description": "Family can be a source of care and harm. This topic treats violence and trauma with analytic seriousness, connecting individual experiences to gender, power, inequality, law, and social services.", "children": [{"id": "fe9fe874-745b-46ef-97b1-44b97f88f7e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intimate partner violence", "children": [{"id": "ec28c383-ce96-4a9a-b836-7a58f89ea129", "type": "concept", "title": "Coercive control"}, {"id": "59ba2e41-6909-4876-b6f1-5450c86bd67b", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender and power"}, {"id": "480c74cd-57e7-46f3-8181-04d44ef2ab98", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal and service systems"}], "description": "Students study patterns of coercive control, violence, barriers to leaving, and institutional response."}, {"id": "a79557c8-ddd0-472a-90b0-85b0d2042195", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Child maltreatment and adverse experiences", "children": [{"id": "5d54d60c-577a-473e-9a10-1b9939639e11", "type": "concept", "title": "Neglect and abuse categories"}, {"id": "ed3b0463-acd8-49bd-b605-acc1d24a76d4", "type": "concept", "title": "ACEs research"}, {"id": "b0224f56-e080-4614-bc49-ef4ce5e5c14a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mandatory reporting and its tensions"}], "description": "Students connect child safety to family stress, resources, trauma, and prevention."}, {"id": "730be424-c6f8-4946-9dfb-6716c5185eff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Trauma-informed and structural perspectives", "children": [{"id": "4cc7b9dc-8105-44f0-8395-edae1e7ebcdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Poverty, housing, and stress"}, {"id": "c6bfe279-b127-4946-9777-853201b0a89f", "type": "concept", "title": "Community violence"}, {"id": "a98deda7-f21f-427e-9d93-301fb9894c3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Prevention and survivor-centered policy"}], "description": "A sociological lens avoids reducing violence to individual pathology alone."}]}, {"id": "fd60217b-06dd-4842-8431-b9dc042fb2d7", "type": "topic", "title": "Family Policy, Law, and the Welfare State", "description": "Families are governed by policies around marriage, taxation, childcare, leave, welfare, immigration, healthcare, education, and eldercare. Students learn how states define and support families unevenly.", "children": [{"id": "1036e108-11ea-4d10-ae30-7b716923423e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Family law and state recognition", "children": [{"id": "8b02b2b7-a0ec-4228-8117-b27484a958e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage and divorce law"}, {"id": "1dc348fc-64d7-4397-bd7a-90c8347788a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Custody and child support"}, {"id": "977a16f9-b726-4385-8741-7ddc84e216b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Adoption and reproductive law"}], "description": "Law determines whose relationships count and what obligations are enforceable."}, {"id": "3da7bf6c-62ca-437c-81a9-74cd8e92b1ed", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Welfare-state supports", "children": [{"id": "79e4f5ba-6e20-4bdc-8b69-5e6577a8e53a", "type": "concept", "title": "Paid family leave"}, {"id": "6319129d-eea9-421a-9c8a-7397a560b063", "type": "concept", "title": "Childcare and child allowances"}, {"id": "f053202b-22fa-4ad8-8652-3535ed998aa9", "type": "concept", "title": "Healthcare, housing, and food assistance"}], "description": "Students compare policies that support or penalize caregiving."}, {"id": "206ebb39-6ba5-4718-9818-57f0d552b62e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Policy debates and unintended consequences", "children": [{"id": "dab07e42-ccbe-42e9-81cb-28855cc8e43b", "type": "concept", "title": "Marriage promotion"}, {"id": "5491439a-e38c-4db5-b967-bf0d15695bb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Work requirements"}, {"id": "dc8b5dcf-b9de-4b02-ad1b-c2a9a292b40f", "type": "concept", "title": "Universal versus targeted benefits"}], "description": "Family policy often reflects moral assumptions and political conflict."}]}, {"id": "23fd45ee-b785-4523-84ae-427bfee3977f", "type": "topic", "title": "Cross-Cultural and Global Family Variation", "description": "Family forms vary across societies, and globalization intensifies comparison, migration, and policy learning. Students study family diversity without ranking cultures on a simplistic scale.", "children": [{"id": "944c3522-ca41-489d-ab44-c0ccf42ac112", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparative family systems", "children": [{"id": "3a39b749-8da8-466a-8064-4c2f264a34c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Patrilineal and matrilineal systems"}, {"id": "c317a28d-b870-4ebb-8943-71307aa2d56c", "type": "concept", "title": "Arranged and love marriage"}, {"id": "614cd96a-495a-4d97-97ee-742b61575e42", "type": "concept", "title": "Extended kin obligations"}], "description": "Students compare marriage, kinship, household, inheritance, and caregiving across contexts."}, {"id": "6afaf4eb-736a-4ff3-927e-9700eb00f8d3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Globalization and family change", "children": [{"id": "6c33355e-779d-427d-9e57-c9bb93ac9a22", "type": "concept", "title": "Global care chains"}, {"id": "dbfc3777-7df0-4941-8e5a-0a8845acf5bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora households"}, {"id": "699bf3f6-8228-4a93-9452-ce3fb702b24f", "type": "concept", "title": "Transnational adoption and reproduction"}], "description": "Economic integration, migration, media, and conflict reshape family life worldwide."}, {"id": "998fe9be-7a1b-4d2a-b990-59260b843eda", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Human rights and family policy", "children": [{"id": "335a08f5-117a-446e-87f7-9b0b3e3a549a", "type": "concept", "title": "UN family and child frameworks"}, {"id": "a0667f38-4b11-4587-96ef-452a4051757f", "type": "concept", "title": "Gender policy comparisons"}, {"id": "f28d0d79-4b7c-48e0-b3ab-53dc7ef93c4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Care infrastructure across welfare regimes"}], "description": "Global family debates often involve gender equality, children's rights, eldercare, and migration."}]}, {"id": "0a054ddc-9c6e-42a5-b6de-d76fc58f1ab4", "type": "topic", "title": "Aging, Intergenerational Relations, and Caregiving", "description": "Longer life expectancy and population aging transform family obligations. Students examine eldercare, grandparenting, inheritance, intergenerational solidarity, and conflict.", "children": [{"id": "ae0d3c4e-37d6-47e5-8240-6f432fdac08c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intergenerational ties", "children": [{"id": "37069da5-4d0c-4dee-91a6-95fdbc700d4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Intergenerational solidarity"}, {"id": "79746e02-5bbb-4a0e-8ecb-8c32cda49253", "type": "concept", "title": "Family conflict"}, {"id": "7fe98808-2b93-4f2f-a75c-59ddb563e1fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Inheritance and wealth transfer"}], "description": "Family relationships unfold across the life course and link generations through care, money, memory, and obligation."}, {"id": "b802a994-42ea-4fc9-ae9b-0267128911bf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Aging and eldercare", "children": [{"id": "05a5d851-1d90-4a8d-806d-000e3cbc7b4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Informal caregiving"}, {"id": "4024a2e3-bfc6-4fde-8596-5a0e9f62de66", "type": "concept", "title": "Long-term care systems"}, {"id": "86bafcd5-9d5d-4898-92b1-915555851261", "type": "concept", "title": "Sandwich generation"}], "description": "Students study how families manage care needs amid unequal resources and limited public supports."}, {"id": "b1e1b827-84e2-4103-86ee-cbdc21e0c084", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Grandparents and kin care", "children": [{"id": "aba31dad-82a8-4975-a23f-bfa13816c626", "type": "concept", "title": "Grandfamilies"}, {"id": "c2b30bbe-4ee2-45c2-827b-79be9866a5fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Custodial grandparenting"}, {"id": "a09946c7-fbe4-4df0-b839-3da1c1e92c19", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural transmission"}], "description": "Grandparents often play central roles in childcare, housing, culture, and family stability."}]}, {"id": "a195ee15-3d1a-4725-990c-d5569ccd8a8a", "type": "topic", "title": "Technology, Media, and Reproductive Futures", "description": "Technology changes dating, fertility, parenting, surveillance, work-family boundaries, and kinship. Students learn to analyze new tools as social arrangements rather than neutral inventions.", "children": [{"id": "5362cff9-43be-48a2-ba67-ca1c95e558ff", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital family life", "children": [{"id": "8a5c41b1-01bf-47de-8b88-5b5bf1145b8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Family group chats"}, {"id": "5cf089fc-6316-4ccc-b735-aaeb6be325be", "type": "concept", "title": "Parenting and screen time"}, {"id": "4d808360-b801-4e12-b9cc-5b59cffd84de", "type": "concept", "title": "Surveillance and privacy"}], "description": "Phones, platforms, and media reshape intimacy, conflict, care, and family communication."}, {"id": "fb7498c4-3523-4790-9418-779922fbf1f2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Assisted reproduction and kinship", "children": [{"id": "ddd535a2-9200-4ace-8631-fdf708cbb213", "type": "concept", "title": "IVF and donor conception"}, {"id": "25be5028-d763-4d9e-9321-074f37f033d5", "type": "concept", "title": "Surrogacy"}, {"id": "c4f9ba4a-2626-4882-8239-e91671757d25", "type": "concept", "title": "Genetic testing and ancestry platforms"}], "description": "Reproductive technologies raise questions about biology, law, markets, inequality, and parenthood."}, {"id": "28dcd7de-a147-467f-99bf-b3f048602c8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Future family debates", "children": [{"id": "38632fd2-c240-4acb-bd40-d5b40b25efe0", "type": "concept", "title": "Remote work and family life"}, {"id": "53a99f92-ced7-4ce6-a49c-e82e6fe32225", "type": "concept", "title": "AI companionship and care technologies"}, {"id": "c90eb271-e82d-49dd-88a8-35d30ca9e15a", "type": "concept", "title": "Bioethics and access inequality"}], "description": "Students evaluate emerging issues without treating novelty as destiny."}]}, {"id": "77a62fd3-cfc0-4215-8f2c-b5bc0bd6a8e8", "type": "topic", "title": "Family Research, Public Scholarship, and Final Synthesis", "description": "The course ends by teaching students to use family research responsibly in public debate. Students synthesize evidence, theory, policy, and lived experience into a rigorous final project.", "children": [{"id": "32a0ee48-4065-48aa-9d2c-f5a4b5e30187", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evaluating public claims about family", "children": [{"id": "782a9414-c7bf-439e-88e3-89ad8e3e445c", "type": "concept", "title": "Media literacy"}, {"id": "744b3c77-2419-450a-9c6a-ad1347c4e30c", "type": "concept", "title": "Research briefs"}, {"id": "34136971-5802-4400-9e59-2d3cb7ff55fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence versus anecdote"}], "description": "Students learn to identify moral panic, weak evidence, and misleading causal claims."}, {"id": "a6310eda-7875-4da1-9540-1b8486b794b0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Designing a family sociology project", "children": [{"id": "31594343-4fc8-4752-b463-4eb91b0cd0f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question and literature review"}, {"id": "919f670c-da85-4648-9b2b-4a3bc405738d", "type": "concept", "title": "Data source selection"}, {"id": "862b4c17-1d05-43ce-b631-15c96cab069c", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethics and positionality"}], "description": "Students translate a family issue into a researchable question and appropriate evidence."}, {"id": "802d8efe-301c-435c-926c-71226e7a63ec", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Final synthesis and communication", "children": [{"id": "b73b2980-8377-4eca-a205-34e0f5495104", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy memo"}, {"id": "eb9c998f-aab5-4a6f-bbdc-1f69f84c9f42", "type": "concept", "title": "Public-facing explainer"}, {"id": "66494004-fe11-440e-a36a-722cf6ad5cda", "type": "concept", "title": "Final research presentation"}], "description": "Students practice communicating family sociology to scholarly and public audiences."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$sofamjson$::jsonb,
